definition:
    American physicist (1901–1958) who invented the cyclotron in 1930 — the first accelerator practical enough to smash atoms productively — winning the 1939 Nobel Prize in Physics. His Radiation Laboratory at Berkeley became the birthplace of accelerator-based science; it spun off the discovery of technetium, plutonium, and hundreds of radioisotopes, and later evolved into today's Lawrence Berkeley and Lawrence Livermore national laboratories. Element 103, lawrencium, is named for him.
